1. What is Spring Boot Actuator?
Spring Boot Actuator is a Spring Boot module that provides production-ready features to monitor, manage, and troubleshoot running applications.

In simple terms, Actuator helps us understand the health, status, performance, and internal metrics of a Spring Boot application without writing separate monitoring APIs.

Real-time example:

Suppose you have deployed a Spring Boot microservice in AWS or Kubernetes. You want to know:
- Is my application running?
- Is the database connection working?
- How much memory is being consumed?
- How many API requests are being processed?
- Are there any application errors?
- Is Kafka or another dependent service available?
Spring Boot Actuator provides built-in endpoints to retrieve this information.


2. Advantages of Spring Boot Actuator
Feature	Purpose
.Health Monitoring	Checks application and dependency health
2.Application Metrics	Monitors CPU, memory, JVM, threads and HTTP requests
3.Production Readiness	Helps verify whether an application can receive traffic
4.Environment Information	Provides application configuration information
5.Dynamic Logging	Changes log levels without restarting the application
6.Application Information	Displays application name, version and build details
7.Bean Inspection	Lists Spring-managed beans
8.Request Monitoring	Tracks API request counts and response times
9.Monitoring Integration	Integrates with Prometheus, Grafana, Datadog, etc.
10.Troubleshooting	Helps identify application and infrastructure issues


3. How to Enable Spring Boot Actuator?
Step 1: Add the Maven dependency
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-actuator</artifactId>
</dependency>


Step 2: Configure Actuator endpoints

In application.properties:
# Expose selected Actuator endpoints
management.endpoints.web.exposure.include=health,info,metrics,beans,mappings

# Display detailed health information
management.endpoint.health.show-details=always

# Enable environment properties for info endpoint
management.info.env.enabled=true

# Application information
info.app.name=Payment Service
info.app.description=Payment Microservice
info.app.version=1.0.0

Step 3: Access the Actuator endpoints
Once the application starts, access:
http://localhost:8080/actuator

This displays the available exposed endpoints.
By default, Spring Boot exposes only the health endpoint over HTTP. The info endpoint must be explicitly exposed to access it over HTTP.

4. Important Actuator Endpoints
Here are the most commonly used endpoints.
Endpoint	Purpose
/actuator	Lists available exposed endpoints
/actuator/health	Checks application health
/actuator/info	Displays application information
/actuator/metrics	Lists available application metrics
/actuator/env	Displays environment properties
/actuator/beans	Lists Spring-managed beans
/actuator/mappings	Lists registered API mappings
/actuator/loggers	Views and modifies logging levels
/actuator/threaddump	Displays thread information
/actuator/heapdump	Generates JVM heap dump
/actuator/prometheus	Exposes metrics in Prometheus format




5. Detailed Explanation of Important Endpoints
5.1 Health Endpoint – /actuator/health
Purpose: Checks whether the application and its dependent components are functioning correctly.

Example:
GET http://localhost:8080/actuator/health

Basic response:
{
  "status": "UP"
}


Possible health statuses:
Status	        Meaning
UP	            Component is functioning correctly
DOWN	        Component has failed
UNKNOWN	        Health status cannot be determined
OUT_OF_SERVICE	Component is unavailable
MAINTENANCE	    Component is undergoing maintenance

How to get detailed health information?
Add this configuration:
management.endpoint.health.show-details=always

Example response:
{
  "status": "UP",
  "components": {
    "db": {
      "status": "UP",
      "details": {
        "database": "PostgreSQL",
        "validationQuery": "isValid()"
      }
    },
    "diskSpace": {
      "status": "UP",
      "details": {
        "total": 320186871808,
        "free": 35127861248,
        "threshold": 10485760
      }
    },
    "ping": {
      "status": "UP"
    }
  }
}


What does it monitor?
- Database connectivity.
- Disk space availability.
- Application responsiveness.
- Messaging system connectivity, when configured.
- Custom health indicators.
For example, if the database goes down, the health endpoint can report:
{
  "status": "DOWN",
  "components": {
    "db": {
      "status": "DOWN"
    }
  }
}


5.2 Info Endpoint – /actuator/info
Purpose: Displays application-related information such as application name, description, version and build details.

By default response is empty json:
{}
The info endpoint returns empty {} by default because Spring Boot 2.6+ disabled the env and java info contributors by default.

ADD Configuration:
management:
  info:
    env:
      enabled: true
    java:
      enabled: true
    os:
      enabled: true

info:
  app:
    name: Job Application Portal
    description: Spring Boot REST API
    version: 1.0.0

Access:
GET http://localhost:8080/actuator/info

Response:
{
  "app": {
    "name": "Job Application Portal",
    "description": "Spring Boot REST API",
    "version": "1.0.0"
  }
}


5.3 Metrics Endpoint – /actuator/metrics
Purpose: Provides application performance and JVM metrics.

Access:
GET http://localhost:8080/actuator/metrics


This displays available metrics.

Some commonly used metrics:
Metric	                    Purpose
jvm.memory.used	            JVM memory currently used
jvm.memory.max	            Maximum JVM memory
jvm.classes.loaded	        Number of loaded classes
jvm.threads.live	        Number of active JVM threads
system.cpu.usage	        System CPU utilization
process.cpu.usage	        Application process CPU utilization
http.server.requests	    HTTP request metrics
process.uptime	            Application uptime

Example 1: Check JVM memory usage
GET /actuator/metrics/jvm.memory.used

Example 2: Check API request metrics
GET /actuator/metrics/http.server.requests

Real-time usage:
Suppose your microservice is experiencing slow API responses.
You can use metrics to investigate request counts, response times, JVM memory consumption and CPU utilization.
Note: Metrics are provided through Micrometer, which is integrated with Spring Boot Actuator.



5.4 Loggers Endpoint – /actuator/loggers
Purpose: Allows developers to view and dynamically change application logging levels without restarting the application.

Supported logging levels:
- TRACE
- DEBUG
- INFO
- WARN
- ERROR
- OFF


Example 1: Check current logging level
GET /actuator/loggers/com.example.payment
Response:
{
  "configuredLevel": "INFO",
  "effectiveLevel": "INFO"
}



Example 2: Change logging level dynamically
POST /actuator/loggers/com.example.payment
Content-Type: application/json
Request body:
{
  "configuredLevel": "DEBUG"
}

Response: 204 No Content
Now the application logs for that package will be generated at DEBUG level and above.
Real-time usage:
Suppose a production API is failing intermittently.
Instead of restarting the application or changing configuration files, developers can temporarily enable DEBUG logging for the relevant package and investigate the issue.


5.5 Mappings Endpoint – /actuator/mappings
Purpose: Displays all registered Spring MVC or WebFlux request mappings.

Access:
GET /actuator/mappings


Example information:
GET /api/users
POST /api/users
GET /api/users/{id}
PUT /api/users/{id}
DELETE /api/users/{id}

Real-time usage:

Useful when verifying whether REST endpoints are registered correctly or troubleshooting API mapping conflicts.


5.6 Environment Endpoint – /actuator/env
Purpose: Displays application environment properties, active profiles and configuration sources.
Access:
GET /actuator/env

It can help identify:
- Active Spring profiles.
- Configuration property sources.
- Environment properties.
- Application configuration values.

Real-time usage:
Suppose your application works in DEV but fails in QA. You can investigate differences in active profiles and configuration properties.

Security consideration
This endpoint can expose sensitive configuration information. Keep it restricted and ensure sensitive values are sanitized.


6. Liveness vs Readiness Probes
These are particularly important when deploying Spring Boot microservices in Kubernetes.

Spring Boot Actuator supports health probes.
Probe	Endpoint	Purpose
Liveness	/actuator/health/liveness	Checks whether the application is alive
Readiness	/actuator/health/readiness	Checks whether the application is ready to receive traffic

Liveness example:
Suppose an application gets stuck due to an internal deadlock.
The liveness probe can detect the unhealthy state and Kubernetes can restart the container.

Readiness example:
Suppose the application has started but is still initializing or establishing required connections.
The readiness probe can indicate that the application is not ready to receive traffic.
Once it becomes ready, Kubernetes can route traffic to it.

Configuration:
management.endpoint.health.probes.enabled=true